---
// Micro module in the site's six steps: find, list, external, internal, weigh, decide.
import MicroModule from '../../components/MicroModule.astro';
import MMStep from '../../components/MMStep.astro';
import MMQuestion from '../../components/MMQuestion.astro';
import Collation from '../../components/Collation.astro';
import * as M from '../../data/collations/mark-1-2';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
const sources = [
  'Bruce M. Metzger and Bart D. Ehrman, <i>The Text of the New Testament</i>, 4th ed. (Oxford University Press, 2005), p. 264.',
  'Manuscript and edition texts: Alan Bunning, Center for New Testament Restoration, transcriptions, CC BY-SA 4.0.',
];
const policy = '<a href="' + url('guide/text-policy/') + '">text policy</a>';
---
<MicroModule title="Mark 1:2: “in Isaiah” or “in the prophets”?" passage="Mark 1:2" minutes={10} sources={sources}
  description="A short guided problem in six steps: a scribe 'corrects' an apparent error in Mark 1:2."
  lead="Mark begins with a quotation and names the prophet it comes from. Some manuscripts name Isaiah; others say only “the prophets”. Work out which came first, in the six steps of the worked examples.">

  <MMStep n={1} title="Find the problem">
    <p class="g">Καθὼς γέγραπται ἐν τῷ Ἠσαΐᾳ τῷ προφήτῃ· Ἰδοὺ ἀποστέλλω τὸν ἄγγελόν μου πρὸ προσώπου σου …</p>
    <p>“As it is written in Isaiah the prophet: ‘Look, I am sending my messenger ahead of you …’” The words that follow in verses 2–3 join two passages: Malachi 3:1 (“I am sending my messenger”) and Isaiah 40:3 (“a voice of one crying in the wilderness”).</p>
    <MMQuestion q="What might trouble a careful scribe about the words “in Isaiah the prophet”?" options={[
      { text: 'The quotation is not only from Isaiah.', correct: true, why: 'Right. It begins with Malachi 3:1. Metzger and Ehrman call it a composite quotation from Malachi and Isaiah (p. 264).' },
      { text: 'Mark should have named Moses.', why: 'No: neither passage is from the books of Moses.' },
      { text: 'Gospel writers never name a prophet.', why: 'No: the Gospels often name the prophet they quote (Matthew 3:3, for example, names Isaiah).' },
    ]} />
  </MMStep>

  <MMStep n={2} title="List the readings">
    <p>Here is how the manuscripts in the CNTR transcriptions, and five printed editions, read the verse. Look at the first card.</p>
    <Collation verse={M.verse} data={M.data_} base={M.base} english={M.english} witnesses={M.witnesses} units={M.units} phrases={M.phrases} glosses={M.glosses} />
    <MMQuestion q="How many different readings are there for the prophet’s name?" options={[
      { text: 'Two: “in Isaiah the prophet” and “in the prophets”.', correct: true, why: 'Right. Codex Bezae (D) writes “in Isaiah the prophet” without “the” before Isaiah, but the meaning is the same, so it belongs with the first reading.' },
      { text: 'Three, because D has no “the”.', why: 'Not really: leaving out the article does not change what is said. Count readings by meaning, not by spelling or small words.' },
      { text: 'One: all the witnesses agree.', why: 'No: the first card shows two groups of witnesses.' },
    ]} />
  </MMStep>

  <MMStep n={3} title="External evidence">
    <p>Now ask the questions about the witnesses: how old they are, where they come from, and how many.</p>
    <MMQuestion q="Which reading do the oldest manuscripts here have, Codex Sinaiticus (א) and Codex Vaticanus (B), both from the 4th century?" options={[
      { text: '“in Isaiah the prophet”', correct: true, why: 'Right. So does Codex Bezae (D, about 400). “In the prophets” is found in A and W (5th century), in the Byzantine text and in the Textus Receptus: later, though more numerous.' },
      { text: '“in the prophets”', why: 'No: that is the reading of A and W (5th century), the Byzantine text and the Textus Receptus.' },
    ]} />
  </MMStep>

  <MMStep n={4} title="Internal evidence">
    <p>Ask what a copyist was likely to do. A scribe who knew the quotation starts with Malachi might think “Isaiah” was a mistake.</p>
    <MMQuestion q="Which change is a scribe more likely to have made?" options={[
      { text: '“in Isaiah the prophet” → “in the prophets”, to avoid naming the wrong prophet.', correct: true, why: 'Right. Metzger and Ehrman: later scribes, sensing a difficulty, replaced “in Isaiah the prophet” with the general statement “in the prophets” (p. 264). This is the “harder reading” rule at work.' },
      { text: '“in the prophets” → “in Isaiah the prophet”, to be more specific.', why: 'Unlikely: that change would create a difficulty, not remove one. Scribes removed difficulties more often than they made them.' },
    ]} />
  </MMStep>

  <MMStep n={5} title="Weigh it up">
    <p>Put the external and internal evidence together.</p>
    <MMQuestion q="Which reading most likely came first?" options={[
      { text: '“in Isaiah the prophet”: the oldest witnesses have it, and it explains why scribes changed it.', correct: true, why: 'Right. Both kinds of evidence point the same way, which is why the UBS text prints this reading.' },
      { text: '“in the prophets”: more manuscripts have it.', why: 'No: number comes last. Many later copies can all go back to one changed ancestor.' },
      { text: 'We cannot tell.', why: 'Here we can: the age of the witnesses and the scribes’ motive agree.' },
    ]} />
  </MMStep>

  <MMStep n={6} title="Decide for your translation" last>
    <p>The UBS text prints “in Isaiah the prophet”. Bibles that follow the Textus Receptus, such as the KJV, have “in the prophets”.</p>
    <MMQuestion q="Your regional-language Bible has “in the prophets”. What is the best next step?" options={[
      { text: 'Take the difference to your Review Committee, recommending the UBS text (“in Isaiah the prophet”) with a footnote.', correct: true, why: 'Right. Your ' + policy + ' asks for the UBS text, and for the Review Committee to decide where the regional-language Bible differs. Our recommendation is the UBS reading with a footnote such as: “Some manuscripts read ‘in the prophets’.” The committee makes the decision.' },
      { text: 'Print “in the prophets” because it is more accurate.', why: 'No: that is exactly the “correction” the scribes made. We translate the earliest text we can reach, not the one we think is more precise.' },
      { text: 'Leave out the name and say “as it is written”.', why: 'No: that reading is not in the manuscripts. Never change the text to remove a difficulty.' },
    ]} />
  </MMStep>
</MicroModule>
